(project pricklypear-lisp-interpreter
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: lisp-interpreter")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section lisp-interpreter
  (doc "Pure Scheme-flavored Lisp-1 interpreter, AST-as-source, primitives layer")
  (status partial)

  (subsection language-roadmap
   (doc "Nopales trajectory phases 1–3: code-as-data law, dialect ergonomics, production capabilities")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "phase exit criteria listed per phase; no phase-N work starts until phase N-1 exit green"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         NORTH STAR
           Nopales is a small Scheme-family impure functional language
           for a live multi-user image (humans + agents + Postgres).
           Not pure FP. Not R7RS-complete. Not a static-typed ML.

         TRAJECTORY (do in order; dogfood between phases)
           Phase 1 — Honest code-as-data
           Phase 2 — Pleasant dialect
           Phase 3 — Production capabilities
           (Phase 4+ modules-lite / hygiene / maps: OUT OF SCOPE here;
            open only after 1–3 exit and real dogfood pain.)

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 1 — HONEST CODE-AS-DATA
         ==========================================================
         Goal: programs-as-values are Scheme-honest; errors are
         debuggable; print/read is law.

         Work items (subsections):
           symbols              VSymbol; non-lossy quote; symbol prims
           errors-and-locations structured errors + source locations
           (reader/eval touch-ups for pp laws — fold into symbols)

         Exit criteria:
           - (quote a) vs (quote "a") distinct (type-of + equality)
           - (car (read-string "(lambda (x) x)")) is symbol?
           - (car (function-ast <lambda-fn>)) is symbol?
           - __str__ not required for new macros
           - Interp errors carry location when expr has span
           - dune runtest lisp green; ui/home + libs/verify smoke green
           - agent seed documents symbol=? for AST walks

         Non-goals this phase: match, define sugar, effect enforcement.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 2 — PLEASANT DIALECT
         ==========================================================
         Goal: humans and LLMs write real packages without fighting
         the dialect every screenful.

         Work items (subsections):
           binding-sugar        (define (f x) …), multi-body/begin
           match                destructuring for lists/AST
           std-library          freeze blessed list/string helpers
           errors-and-locations catch/guard surface (builds on P1)

         Exit criteria:
           - (define (f x y) body…) ≡ (define f (lambda (x y) body…))
           - multi-expr procedure bodies via begin/seq sugar
           - (match x ((list 'lambda params body) …) (_ …))
             or equivalent documented destructuring
           - std: map/filter/foldl/reverse/append/… documented +
             verify suite; no silent renames after freeze
           - (guard …) or with-handlers can catch User_error
           - one package (e.g. flash or calendar widget) rewritten
             to use sugar+match as the style exemplar

         Non-goals: modules, hygiene, maps, static types.

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 3 — PRODUCTION CAPABILITIES
         ==========================================================
         Goal: untrusted surfaces cannot casually escalate; packages
         have a promised API; agents run under allowlists.

         Work items (subsections):
           effects              extend: ENFORCE not only badge
           capability-enforcement public routes + agent sessions
           package-surface      blessed exports / stability rules
           (budgets already exist — document + align timeouts here)

         Exit criteria:
           - public user routes rejected (or sandboxed) if handler
             effects ⊄ allowlist (pure|read-db|html|… policy table)
           - agent tool / agent-say path has configurable effect mask
           - AI propose-function still policy-gated (already started)
           - system/main packages declare export list OR documented
             public names; breaking changes require promote discipline
           - smoke: non-admin cannot publish WriteDb+Branch handler
             as public route; agent without WriteDb cannot create row
             via tool when mask forbids it
           - habitat verify + auth smokes green

         Non-goals: setuid-as-user lattice completion, OCaml 5
         Effect.perform in guest Lisp, full RBAC redesign.

         ==========================================================
         ORDERING LAW
         ==========================================================
           Do not start Phase 2 implementation until Phase 1 exit.
           Do not start Phase 3 enforcement until Phase 2 exit
           (sugar may land in libs that enforcement will load).
           Spec for all three may be written now (this pass).

         ==========================================================
         DECISION DEFAULTS (locked for 1–3)
         ==========================================================
           - Impure functional Scheme-family dialect
           - Dynamic typing + effects + data schemas (not HM types)
           - Unhygienic macros + gensym (no syntax-case in 1–3)
           - No call/cc
           - No in-Lisp fiber/async API (host owns concurrency)
           - Branches remain the override model; modules-lite = P4+
           - JSON/dict prims for records; no VMap required in 1–3
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection ast
   (doc "The expr and value types")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         type expr =
           | Var of string
           | Lit of value
           | App of expr * expr list
           | Let of string * expr * expr
           | Letrec of (string * expr) list * expr
           | If of expr * expr * expr
           | Lambda of string list * expr
           | Prim of string * expr list
           | Seq of expr * expr
           | Quote of expr                 (* data quote; see macros *)
           | Quasiquote of expr            (* macros.quasiquote *)
           | Unquote of expr
           | UnquoteSplicing of expr

         type value =
           | VInt of int64
           | VFloat of float
           | VStr of string
           | VSymbol of string               (* see subsection symbols *)
           | VBool of bool
           | VNil
           | VPair of value * value           (* cons cells *)
           | VLambda of string list * expr * env
           | VMacro of string list * expr * env
               (* defmacro transformer; params bind to UNEVALUATED
                  arg exprs reified as values via quote_to_value;
                  body returns a value tree that value_to_expr
                  turns back into an expr for re-eval. See macros. *)
           | VPrim of string                  (* primitive name *)
           | VRef of type_name * id           (* tagged ref into store *)
           | VList of value list              (* sugar for nested pairs *)

         The AST IS the source (homoiconicity). Stored as
         JSONB in function_defs.body. The reader (s-expr
         parser) produces expr values; the printer
         round-trips them. No parser/printer asymmetry.

         Closures capture their defining env (lexical
         scope). VLambda carries env. No dynamic scope.

         VRef carries its target type so the interpreter
         can verify derefs at call time before querying
         the store.

         VALUE ATOMS vs CODE NAMES:
           At the expr layer, Var s and Lit (VStr s) are already
           distinct. At the value layer, v1 collapsed both to VStr
           ("historical collapse"). Subsection symbols plans the
           move to VSymbol so quote/read-string/function-ast trees
           match Scheme-family code-as-data.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection symbols
   (doc "Distinguish symbols from strings at the value layer (undo v1 collapse)")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "quote hello is symbol; quote \"hello\" is string; not equal")
    (test "read-string and function-ast heads are symbols (car is symbol?)")
    (test "value_to_expr round-trip preserves Var vs Lit VStr without __str__")
    (test "gensym returns VSymbol; symbol->string / string->symbol")
    (test "string-eq rejects symbols; symbol-eq compares names")
    (smoke "(function-ast \"ui/nav\") car is lambda symbol; ui/home still renders")
    (smoke "agent seed: read-string + car/cdr walk still works with symbol-eq or as-str"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHY (decision 2026-07-24)
           v1 accepted symbol/string collapse so the value heap stayed
           tiny and HTML/JSON APIs stayed stringly. That was right for
           shipping the image. It is wrong as the steady state for a
           homoiconic agent OS: macros, function-ast, read-string, and
           LLM-written code all want Scheme's distinction.

           Spec previously said "optional later: VSymbol". This section
           promotes that to a planned language change with phases.

         GOAL
           (quote hello)     => VSymbol "hello"
           (quote "hello")   => VStr "hello"
           (eq? (quote a) (quote a))     ; name equality on symbols
           (string-eq "a" "a")           ; strings only
           (symbol? 'lambda) => #t
           (string? "lambda") => #t
           (car (read-string "(lambda (x) x)")) => symbol lambda
           (car (function-ast "f")) when f is a lambda => symbol lambda

         NON-GOALS (v1 of symbols)
           - Full interning table / eq identity across processes
             (name equality on the string payload is enough;
              process-local intern optional later)
           - Keyword type (:foo) — out of scope
           - Changing expr JSONB shape (Var/Lit already distinct)
           - Forcing all libs to rewrite in one patch

         ==========================================================
         TYPE + PRINT/READ
         ==========================================================

         OCaml:
           | VSymbol of string

         JSON value encoding (if/when values are serialized):
           {"sym": "hello"} vs {"str": "hello"}
           expr JSON already has {"var":…} vs {"str":…} — leave it.

         Printer (pp_value / REPL / api/eval JSON):
           VSymbol s  ->  s          (unquoted bare token, escape if needed)
           VStr s     ->  "s"        (double-quoted, escaped)
         Reader:
           bare token -> Var (unchanged at expr)
           "…"        -> Lit VStr (unchanged)
         Quote bridge is where the new type appears (see below).

         ==========================================================
         BRIDGE RULES (replace collapse)
         ==========================================================

         quote_to_value:
           Var s           -> VSymbol s
           Lit (VStr s)    -> VStr s
           Lit other       -> other
           App/specials    -> VList with symbolic heads where heads
                              were Vars (VSymbol "if", VSymbol "lambda",
                              VSymbol "+", …)

         value_to_expr:
           VSymbol s       -> Var s
           VStr s          -> Lit (VStr s)     (* ALWAYS lit, not Var *)
           VList (VSymbol h :: t) -> App/special decode as today
           VList (VStr h :: t) -> prefer treat h as symbol name only
             for a short COMPAT window (see phase B); then error or
             require explicit string->symbol

         DROP __str__ tagging as the primary mechanism:
           reify_expr / macro arg bind use quote_to_value only.
           __str__ unwrap in coerce_str stays one release for old
           macro expansions in flight, then delete.

         gensym:
           returns VSymbol (not VStr). Optional prefix still string.

         ==========================================================
         PRIMITIVES
         ==========================================================

         New (pure):
           (symbol? x) -> bool
           (string? x) -> bool   (* VStr only; not symbols *)
           (symbol=? a b) -> bool   (* both symbols, name= *)
           (symbol->string sym) -> VStr
           (string->symbol s) -> VSymbol

         Update:
           (type-of x) -> "symbol" | "str" | …
           (str x) / pp: symbols bare, strings quoted (already ideal)
           (as-str x): ACCEPT VSymbol (name payload) for HTML/path
             convenience — product APIs stay ergonomic
           (string-eq a b): strings only (do NOT coerce symbols
             silently forever — that re-collapses). During compat,
             may accept symbol via as-str only inside html/* if needed;
             prefer libs use symbol=? for car walks.
           string-append / substring / etc.: strings only; symbols
             must go through symbol->string

         ==========================================================
         PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase A — Core (lisp package, no product break if pp hides it)
           1. Add VSymbol to ast.ml; pp_value; expr_of_json/value JSON
              if any; type-of; equality helpers.
           2. quote_to_value: Var -> VSymbol; Lit VStr stays VStr.
           3. value_to_expr: VSymbol -> Var; VStr -> Lit VStr.
           4. gensym -> VSymbol.
           5. Tests: quote distinct; round-trip without __str__;
              read_one + quote_to_value heads are symbols.
           6. Keep coerce_str accepting VSymbol and __str__ lists
              so html/* and string-eq call sites do not mass-break.

         Phase B — Surface prims + agent API
           1. symbol? string? symbol=? symbol->string string->symbol.
           2. function-ast / read-string naturally emit symbols
              (they already go through quote_to_value).
           3. Agent discovery / ai seeds: document car heads are
              symbols; show (symbol=? (car form) 'lambda) and
              (string-eq (symbol->string (car form)) "lambda").
           4. Compat: string-eq may still coerce VSymbol via as-str
              for one deploy cycle if smoke finds package damage;
              log or count coercions.

         Phase C — Cleanup
           1. Remove __str__ reify path and tests that require it.
           2. Stop string-eq coercing symbols (strict).
           3. value_to_expr: VStr in head position is Lit-only;
              building code from string heads needs string->symbol.
           4. Update locked macros decision: collapse REJECTED.
           5. Habitat smoke: ui/home, notes, flash, calendar, verify.

         ==========================================================
         PACKAGE / AGENT IMPACT
         ==========================================================

         Low risk (as-str / coerce_str accepts symbols):
           html/tag, html/attr, layout/*, most UI string building.

         Medium risk (explicit string-eq on car of forms):
           any macro or agent walker that does
             (string-eq (car form) "lambda")
           Fix pattern:
             (symbol=? (car form) 'lambda)
             OR (string-eq (as-str (car form)) "lambda") during B.

         function-ast / read-string consumers:
           printed api/eval JSON will show bare lambda not "lambda"
           for heads — agents parsing JSON strings need a heads-up.

         Stored function_defs JSONB:
           unchanged (expr encoding). No migration of bodies.

         ==========================================================
         ROLLBACK
         ==========================================================

           Feature is pure interpreter + prims. Revert the lisp/image
           commit if habitat smoke fails; no DB migration.

         ==========================================================
         SUCCESS
         ==========================================================

           - 'a and "a" are different values (type-of + equality)
           - code-as-data trees use symbols for names
           - macros need no __str__
           - product home and libs/verify green
           - agent docs teach symbol=? for AST walks

         ROADMAP: Phase 1 item 1 — see language-roadmap.
         Pair with errors-and-locations in the same phase.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection errors-and-locations
   (doc "Structured interpreter errors with source locations; catch surface for packages")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "parse error includes line:col")
    (test "unbound var error includes name and optional span")
    (test "guard/with-handlers catches User_error and continues")
    (smoke "/api/eval error JSON includes loc fields when available"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 1 (locations) + Phase 2 (catch surface).

         ==========================================================
         PROBLEM
         ==========================================================
           Today many failures are bare strings / Interp_error without
           a stable shape. Agents and humans cannot reliably:
             - jump to the offending form
             - catch and recover in Lisp
             - distinguish unbound vs type vs prim vs budget

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 1 — ERROR VALUES + LOCATIONS
         ==========================================================

         A) Keep OCaml Interp_error as the host signal, but ensure
            every raise site can carry optional location:
              type loc = { source: string option; line: int; col: int }
                (* source = function name, "repl", "load-string", path *)

         B) Reader already has line/col — propagate into Parse_error
            and into expr spans IF cheap (optional span on expr is
            a larger change; minimum is error-time loc from reader
            state + current define name from session).

         C) /api/eval error JSON (and REPL print):
              { ok: false, error: "unbound: foo",
                kind: "unbound_var", name: "foo",
                line: 12, col: 4, source: "ui/home" }
            Fields optional when unknown; never break old clients
            that only read .error string.

         D) Budget_exceeded, arity, type mismatch get stable kind
            tags (snake_case strings).

         ==========================================================
         PHASE 2 — CATCH IN LISP
         ==========================================================

         Special form (pick one name; prefer guard):

           (guard
             (lambda (err)  ; err is a dict-like or tagged list
               …handler value or re-raise…)
             body…)

         Or Scheme-ish:
           (with-handlers ((pred handler) …) body…)

         v1 contract:
           - Catches User_error and Prim_error wrapped as data
           - Does NOT catch Budget_exceeded by default (or only
             if pred says so) — budgets stay a hard leash
           - Handler runs in the same env; must return a value
           - Nested guard OK

         (error msg) or (error kind msg) prim/special:
           raises User_error with optional kind for handlers.

         ==========================================================
         RELATED — image.dnu-stubs (product holes, not catch)
         ==========================================================
         doesNotUnderstand-style stub+callers UX lives in
         image.dnu-stubs. It reuses unbound_var kind/name fields
         from Phase 1 JSON but does not replace guard/catch.
         Coordinate: stable unbound_var shape first; stubs second.

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Full CL condition system / restarts
           - Stack traces as first-class list (nice-to-have later)
           - Catching OCaml segfaults / host bugs

         ==========================================================
         SUCCESS
         ==========================================================
           Phase 1: every common eval failure has kind + message;
           locations present for read/load-string paths.
           Phase 2: a lib can guard a fallible get/parse and
           render a friendly HTML error without killing the page.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection reader
   (doc "The reader turns source text into AST; the AST is the source of truth (homoiconic, persisted as JSONB); read/print roundtrip fidelity is what makes stored code trustworthy.")
   (status implemented)

   (details
    (doc (|
         The reader parses Lisp surface syntax to expr
         values. Standard s-expressions: (foo bar baz),
         'symbol, "string", 123, #t, #f, ().

         Surface syntax is just an editor concern: a
         function stored in Postgres is its AST (JSONB),
         and the s-expr syntax is one of several possible
         editors (along with the htmx tree-editor that
         edits the AST directly).

         Special forms recognized at read or eval time
         (decision: eval time, the reader is generic):
           (lambda (args) body)
           (let ((name val)...) body)
           (letrec ((name val)...) body)
           (if test then else)
           (define name expr)        (* top-level binding *)
           (quote x)
           (quasiquote x)            (* macros; sugar `x *)
           (unquote x)               (* macros; sugar ,x *)
           (unquote-splicing x)      (* macros; sugar ,@x *)
           (defmacro name (params) body)  (* macros *)
           (deftype name (fields ...))
           (fork parent child)
           (merge source target)
           (app "prim-name" args)    (* explicit prim call *)

         Reader sugar for macros (in addition to 'x -> (quote x)):
           `x   -> (quasiquote x)
           ,x   -> (unquote x)
           ,@x  -> (unquote-splicing x)
         Comma forms are only meaningful under quasiquote; bare
         unquote at top-level is a reader-or-eval error.

         Comments: ; to end of line.

         Docstrings (see define-docstring): optional metadata on
         a top-level binding. Surface forms the reader already
         produces as plain AST (no dedicated constructor):
           (define name expr "doc")
           (define (name p…) "doc" body…)
           (define name (lambda (p…) "doc" body…))
         The string is not a value binding; eval and the image
         strip it into function_defs.doc (column already on
         storage.schema).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection eval
   (doc "The evaluator: env * expr -> value")
   (implements lisp/lib/env.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml)
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest — lisp eval + TCO cases")
    (smoke "tail self-recursion depth >> OCaml stack without overflow")
    (smoke "non-tail recursion still correct (e.g. factorial)")
    (smoke "infinite tail loop hits Budget_exceeded, not hang/stack"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The evaluator is PURE. It takes an env and an expr,
         returns a value or raises an interpreter error.
         No I/O in the interpreter. All side effects go
         through Prim calls, which the IMAGE layer wires to
         actual Postgres/SSH/HTTP side-effects.

         Cooperative evaluation: the eval loop is stepped;
         each Prim invocation yields back to the image's
         event loop. This gives per-session budgeting:
         - A step counter per call (default_limit 100_000)
         - When exceeded, that ONE call aborts
         - The session continues
         - (budget-remaining) reads the active call's leftover
           steps (dynamic current_budget)
         - OCaml prim bodies do not tick; only the call site
         This prevents "user's bad function wedges the
         image" entirely. Scale bugs are fixed by cheaper
         paths (notes.budget-scale), not by raising the limit.

         (define name expr) at top-level adds binding to
         current session's env AND persists to store. Edit
         = redefine (update the row). No versioning at the
         binding level beyond what branches provide.

         Optional docstring (define-docstring): trailing string
         after the value, or leading string in a multi-body
         define/lambda, is accepted by eval (not bound as the
         value) and persisted by the image into function_defs.doc.

         Application: evaluate operator + args, apply.
         Primitives are dispatched by name through a table
         the IMAGE layer registers at boot. The interpreter
         knows nothing about Postgres or htmx.

         Special forms handled in eval, not reader: lambda,
         let, letrec, if, define, quote, quasiquote,
         unquote, unquote-splicing, defmacro, plus higher-order
         list forms list/map, list/filter, list/foldl (OCaml
         loops + apply — not recursive Lisp frames). The reader
         produces App/Variants of these as plain AST (or the
         dedicated Quote/Quasiquote/Unquote constructors when
         sugar is used — either representation is fine as long
         as quote_to_value / expand / pp round-trip).

         Macro expansion is part of eval application (and of
         define/load — see macros.expansion-timing). Eval must
         call expand before ordinary arg evaluation when the
         operator resolves to a VMacro.

         =========================================================
         TAIL-CALL OPTIMIZATION (TCO)
         =========================================================
         Proper Scheme-style TCO via an explicit trampoline in
         eval.ml (OCaml does not guarantee TCO on recursive calls).

         Model:
           eval_step returns Done value | Tail (env, expr)
           eval / eval_force runs a loop: while Tail, continue
           without growing the OCaml stack.

         Tail positions (must bounce, not OCaml-recurse):
           - if/cond: chosen branch
           - and/or: last subform (and short-circuit non-tails force)
           - seq: last form; earlier forms forced
           - let / letrec: body
           - lambda application: body after binding args
             (the App node is always tail in its own frame;
              nested Apps as arguments are forced first)
           - macro expansion result: evaluated in tail position
           - list/map|filter|foldl: stay OCaml loops; closure
             bodies use eval_force (each element is non-tail
             relative to the list form)

         Non-tail positions force to a value (may use OCaml stack
         for the force frame only, not for the tail chain inside).

         Budget: every step still ticks. Infinite tail loops hit
         Budget_exceeded — never hang, never exhaust OS stack.

         Effects: Prim_call still perform; after return, continue
         the trampoline. Rim re-entry (with-transaction,
         apply_lambda) uses the same eval_force entry point.

         Not required: full CPS, custom VM, or rest-args frames.
         Heap env frames still allocate per call (TCO saves stack).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection macros
   (doc "User-defined syntactic macros: defmacro, quasiquote, value<->expr bridge, expand-at-define")
   (status partial)
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/reader.ml lisp/lib/ast.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp")
    (test "dune runtest -- lisp  — macro/quasiquote/value_to_expr cases")
    (smoke "(defmacro when (t b) `(if ,t ,b ())) then (when #t 1) => 1")
    (smoke "top-level defmacro persists; reload expands call sites on load"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. WHY MACROS, WHY NOW
         ==========================================================

         Philosophy parked macros ("can come later"). The habitat
         is past that: product packages, AI authoring, and agents
         writing Lisp all want surface sugar without growing the
         OCaml special-form table forever. Homoiconicity is already
         real (AST-as-JSONB); macros are the missing half — code
         that writes code as data, then re-enters eval.

         Goals for v1:
           - Users/agents can define (when / unless / and / or …)
             in Lisp packages without OCaml changes
           - Macro bodies use quasiquote so templates are readable
           - Expansion is inspectable (macroexpand-1)
           - Persisted function bodies remain readable source ASTs
             (macro CALL sites stored; expand on load/define)
           - No hygiene engine; gensym for accidental capture

         Non-goals for v1:
           - Hygienic macros / syntax-case / syntax-rules
           - Reader macros / custom dispatch chars
           - Compiler-style whole-program expand
           - Separate macro package object type (macros are
             ordinary bindings of value VMacro on a branch)
           - Macro-defining macros (allowed if it falls out;
             not a success criterion)
           - Changing the Lwt eval ABI

         ==========================================================
         1. SURFACE SYNTAX
         ==========================================================

         (defmacro name (p1 p2 …) body)
           - Special form (name not evaluated; params not evaluated)
           - Binds name in the CURRENT env to a VMacro
           - Top-level defmacro ALSO persists like define:
             image layer writes function_defs (or equivalent)
             with a kind/tag distinguishing macro from lambda
             so reload reinstalls VMacro, not VLambda.
             v1 acceptable encoding: store body AST as
               (defmacro name (params) body)  OR
               body with metadata flag is_macro=true.
             Prefer a boolean/kind on function_defs if a column
             already exists for kind; else store the defmacro
             wrapper AST so load can detect head=defmacro.
           - Returns the VMacro (or name string) — mirror define's
             "return the bound value" convention unless image
             layer already normalizes define returns.

         (quote x) / 'x
           - Unchanged: expr -> value via quote_to_value

         (quasiquote template) / `template
           - Evaluates template structure, interpolating unquotes
           - Outside any quasiquote, bare unquote is an error

         (unquote x) / ,x
           - Only valid inside quasiquote; evaluates x in the
             current env and splices the resulting VALUE into
             the template as a single element

         (unquote-splicing x) / ,@x
           - Only valid inside quasiquote list position
           - x must evaluate to a VList (or proper list of pairs);
             elements are spliced into the parent list
           - Error if used in non-list / atomic template position

         (macroexpand-1 form)
           - Special form OR pure prim: if form is (name . args)
             and name is bound to VMacro, run ONE expansion step
             and return the expansion as a value tree (quoted
             shape). If not a macro call, return form unchanged
             (as value via quote_to_value).
           - Does not eval the expansion. (Current impl: evals macro body to produce the value tree; matches inbox language-design note c11-4013 contract for REPL inspection.)

         (macroexpand form)
           - Repeat macroexpand-1 until fixed point or budget
             tick limit; return final value tree.

         (gensym) / (gensym prefix)
           - Pure prim: returns a fresh symbol name as VStr
             (e.g. "g$42" or "prefix$42"). Counter is process-
             local (image or lisp package ref cell). Enough for
             unhygienic capture avoidance in v1.

         ==========================================================
         2. VALUE <-> EXPR BRIDGE (the hard kernel)
         ==========================================================

         Already exists:
           quote_to_value : expr -> value
             Lit v -> v
             Var s -> VStr s
             App/special constructors -> nested VList with
               head symbol strings ("if", "lambda", …)

         REQUIRED for macros:
           value_to_expr : value -> expr
             Inverse, best-effort, for trees macros produce.

         Macro parameter contract (updated):
           Macro arguments are bound as self-evaluated literal VALUES
           (plain VStr for strings, VInt/VFloat/etc. for numbers).
           No __str__ tagging at the macro call boundary.
           The __str__ emit workaround is deprecated.

         Rules (v1):
           VInt/VFloat/VBool/VNil/VStr that are self-evaluating
             literals -> Lit v
           VStr s  used in OPERATOR / BINDING / PARAM position
             of a list tree -> Var s
             (i.e. when value_to_expr sees VList (VStr head :: args),
              head becomes Var head unless head is a known self-
              quoting literal form — see below)
           VList [] -> Lit VNil   (* empty list *)
           VList (VStr "quote" :: [x]) -> Quote (value_to_expr x)
             or App(Var "quote", …) — either OK if eval handles both
           VList (h :: t) -> App (value_to_expr h, map value_to_expr t)
           VPair chain treated like list if proper; improper pair
             error or dotted App — v1: require proper lists from
             macros; raise User_error on improper
           VLambda / VMacro / VPrim / VRef as template leaves:
             error (cannot reify live closures into source expr)

         Symbol problem (Lisp-1 + VSymbol — see subsection symbols):
           v1 collapsed quote of hello and "hello" both to VStr.
           That is SUPERSEDED: planned VSymbol makes
             (quote hello) => VSymbol "hello"
             (quote "hello") => VStr "hello"
           Macro args use quote_to_value (symbols for Vars, strings
           for Lit VStr). __str__ tagging is transitional only until
           symbols phase C removes it.
           Until symbols ships, status quo remains VStr collapse.

         Round-trip invariant (tests):
           For any expr e built from reader surface (no live
           closures): value_to_expr (quote_to_value e) is
           alpha-equivalent / pp-equal enough to eval the same
           for pure programs. Exact AST equality preferred where
           constructors align.

         ==========================================================
         3. EXPANSION SEMANTICS
         ==========================================================

         A VMacro is (params, body_expr, defining_env).

         When applying a macro (name a1 a2 … an):
           1. Do NOT eval a1…an.
           2. Reify each ai with quote_to_value -> vi
           3. Bind params to vi in a child of defining_env
              (lexical macro env — captures helpers at defmacro)
           4. Eval body in that env -> expansion_value
           5. expansion_expr = value_to_expr expansion_value
           6. Eval expansion_expr in the ORIGINAL call-site env
              (not the macro's defining env) — standard Lisp
              macro call-site evaluation of the expansion.

         Recursive expansion:
           If expansion_expr is itself a macro call, expand again
           (macroexpand loop) subject to the same Budget.t as
           eval. Infinite macro recursion => Budget_exceeded.

         Shadowing:
           If a local binding shadows a macro name with a non-
           macro value, App uses ordinary application.
           is_special_form still wins over macros for the fixed
           kernel names (lambda, if, quote, defmacro, …).
           defmacro names MUST NOT be allowed to override kernel
           special forms; define/defmacro of those names errors.

         ==========================================================
         4. EXPANSION TIMING (define / load / REPL)
         ==========================================================

         Two moments matter:

         A) Interactive / one-shot eval (REPL, /api/eval):
            Expand at use (call time), as in §3. Simple and
            correct for ad-hoc forms.

         B) Top-level (define name body) and image load of
            function_defs:
            After reading body AST, run a FULL macroexpand on
            the body BEFORE storing the evaluated VLambda in
            the session env. Rationale:
              - Call sites stay fast (already expanded in the
                in-memory lambda body)
              - Effect inference (lisp-interpreter.effects) sees
                real prims, not macro heads
              - AI/tools that read env closures see expanded code

         Persistence choice (IMPORTANT):
           Store the SOURCE body AST (with macro call sites),
           not only the expanded form.
             function_defs.body = source (what user/agent wrote)
             On session install / load-library / define:
               expand(source) -> eval -> VLambda in env
           Why: tree editor, pp, package export (lib.pp), and
           AI rewrite tools must show what the author wrote.
           Expanded-only storage would destroy macro use sites.

           Optional later cache column expanded_body — not v1.

         defmacro persistence:
           On load, re-bind VMacro from stored defmacro AST
           BEFORE expanding other defines that might call it.
           Install order on a branch:
             1. all macros (defmacro forms)
             2. then functions (define forms), each expanded
                against the macro env so far
           Within a single load batch, dependency order may be
           imperfect; document that macros should be defined
           before use in packages. v1 may expand with whatever
           macros are already in env (seed order / name order).
           Package seed should list macro files/bindings first
           when this matters.

         ==========================================================
         5. QUASIQUOTE EVALUATION MODEL
         ==========================================================

         quasiquote is a special form, not a macro on quote.

         qq(depth, template):
           depth starts at 1 when entering (quasiquote …)
           - (unquote e) at depth=1 -> eval e
           - (unquote e) at depth>1 -> rebuild unquote wrapper
             with qq(depth-1, e)  (nested quasiquote support)
           - (quasiquote e) -> rebuild with qq(depth+1, e)
           - (unquote-splicing e) at depth=1, list context ->
             eval e, must be list, splice
           - atoms / Vars / Lits at any depth -> quote_to_value
           - lists: map qq over elements; splicing flattens

         v1 MUST support single-level quasiquote well; nested
         quasiquote depth counting is required for correctness
         of macros-that-write-macros but can be tested lightly.

         Reader:
           `  introduces quasiquote of next expr
           ,  introduces unquote of next expr
           ,@ introduces unquote-splicing of next expr
         Prefer producing App(Var "quasiquote",[e]) etc. so
         existing App special-form path works; dedicated AST
         constructors (Quasiquote/Unquote/UnquoteSplicing) are
         allowed if JSON + pp + quote_to_value are updated in
         lockstep. Pick ONE representation in implementation
         and use it consistently (recommend App form for less
         JSON schema churn; dedicated constructors OK if cleaner).

         ==========================================================
         6. INTERACTION WITH EFFECTS / AI / BRANCHES
         ==========================================================

         Effects (lisp-interpreter.effects):
           - infer_effects runs on EXPANDED bodies when possible
           - quote / quasiquote templates contribute no runtime
             effects except through unquote subexpressions
           - defmacro body effects are Meta/code-time (top-level define persists via the session layer); the
             expanded call site carries the real effects
           - prim_effects: gensym = Pure; macroexpand* = Meta/Pure

         AI authoring (ai-authoring):
           - propose-function may emit defmacro and macro call
             sites; freeze path stores source AST as today
           - ast-rewrite tools edit source AST; expansion is
             re-done on next define/load

         Branches:
           Macros are bindings; they inherit/override via the
           same function_defs branch visibility as functions.
           A personal branch can override a system macro.

         ==========================================================
         7. ERRORS
         ==========================================================

           - arity mismatch on macro params (like lambda)
           - value_to_expr failure -> User_error with context
           - unquote outside quasiquote
           - unquote-splicing of non-list
           - defmacro/define of kernel special-form name
           - macro expansion budget exceeded
           - improper list from macro body (v1)

         ==========================================================
         8. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 1 — Bridge + quasiquote (no defmacro yet)
           - value_to_expr + round-trip tests with quote_to_value
           - reader sugar ` , ,@
           - eval quasiquote / unquote / unquote-splicing
           - special_forms catalog entries
           - gensym prim

         Phase 2 — defmacro + call-time expand
           - VMacro value
           - defmacro special form
           - App path: if operator evals to VMacro, expand
             (note: operator is a Var lookup without evaluating
              args — for (mac …) head is Var; do not eval head
              as function-before-expand in a way that forces
              args. Pattern: lookup Var name; if VMacro, expand;
              else existing eval head + eval args + apply.)
           - macroexpand-1 / macroexpand
           - tests: when/unless style macros

         Phase 3 — define/load expand + persistence
           - expand body on define before env install of lambdas
           - image load order: macros first
           - persist defmacro so reload restores VMacro
           - package seed: allow macros in libs/*

         Phase 4 — Product sugar (optional same PR / follow-up)
           - stdlib macros: when, unless, and, or, begin~seq alias
             if desired — only if they do not fight existing prims
           - UI: show macro badge on /bindings (kind=macro)

         ==========================================================
         9. SUCCESS CRITERIA
         ==========================================================

         - (defmacro when (t b) `(if ,t ,b ())) works in REPL
         - (when #t 42) => 42; (when #f 42) => ()
         - ,@ splices: `(a ,@'(b c) d) => (a b c d) as value list
         - quote/value_to_expr round-trip tests green
         - defmacro survives image restart / branch checkout
         - define of a function using macros expands so effects
           badges see real prims
         - dune build lisp + dune runtest lisp pass
         - No regression to pure-prim Lwt ABI

         ==========================================================
         10. DECISIONS LOCKED BY THIS SPEC
         ==========================================================

         - Name: defmacro (not define-macro)
         - Hygiene: unhygienic + gensym (no syntax-case)
         - Expansion: call-time always; ALSO expand-at-define/load
           for installed lambdas; store SOURCE AST
         - Symbol/string collapse was accepted for v1; SUPERSEDED by
           subsection symbols (planned VSymbol). Do not build new
           macros that rely on collapse or __str__.
         - Quasiquote is a special form, not a user macro
         - Kernel special forms cannot be redefined as macros
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection primitives
   (doc "Primitives are the boundary between the pure interpreter and the outside world; pure prims are wrapped via register_sync (results in Lwt.return); DB-touching prims are normal prim_fns returning value Lwt.t. The impure functions the interpreter calls into")
   (status partial)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Primitives live in image/lib/primitives.ml. They
         are REAL OCaml, typed at the boundary, fast.
         Anything that touches Postgres, htmx, or the OS
         is a primitive, never user-Lisp.

         CRUD:
           (create 'type {fields...}) -> VRef
           (get 'type id) -> record value
           (update 'type id {fields...}) -> VRef
           (delete 'type id) -> VNil
           (select-for-update 'type id) -> record (locked)

         Branching:
           (fork parent child-name) -> branch-id
           (merge source target) -> status
           (current-branch) -> branch-id
           (checkout branch-id) -> VNil (* session switches *)
           (list-branches) -> VList
           (diff source target) -> VList of changes

         htmx/Routes:
           (route path fn)            (* register route *)
           (render-html node)          (* pure-html node *)
           (hx-get url) (hx-post url) (* attribute helpers *)

         Reflection:
           (type-of value) -> type-tag


           (list-bindings) -> VList

         Sessions:
           (whoami) -> username
           (current-author) -> username (* for perm checks *)

         All primitives that touch data consult the
         CURRENT-AUTHOR (from the binding being executed)
         for permission filtering, per multi-user model.

         Effect tags for each prim are defined in
         lisp-interpreter.effects (capability lattice).
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection effects
   (doc "Capability effects for Lisp programs: classify pure vs DB vs AI vs publish; infer; gate; display. Separate from OCaml 5 host effects and from Lwt async plumbing.")
   (implements lisp/lib/effect.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml image/lib/prims_pure.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (test "effect inference pure vs db vs ai on sample ASTs")
    (smoke "/bindings shows effect badges; AI propose rejects disallowed effects when policy on"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. THREE PROBLEMS PEOPLE CONFUSE (read this first)
         ==========================================================

         A) ASYNC / COMPOSITION  (mostly DONE)
            Can a function call (get ...) and use the result?
            YES today:
              (define u (get 'note id))
              (dict-get u "title")
            Mechanism: eval : env -> expr -> value Lwt.t
            Pure prims: register_sync wraps Lwt.return
            DB/AI prims: return promises directly
            This is the "async problem" for data-in-Lisp.
            It is runtime plumbing, not an effect system.

         B) CAPABILITY EFFECTS  (NOT DONE — this section)
            What powers does this function need?
            Pure calc vs read DB vs write DB vs AI vs publish page.
            Needed for: UI badges, AI safety, page policy,
            testing without services, author-runs-as-author story.

         C) HOST ALGEBRAIC EFFECTS (OPTIONAL LATER)
            OCaml 5 Effect.perform / handlers inside the
            interpreter implementation. Could one day replace
            some Lwt coloring. Does NOT give Lisp programs
            effects for free. Guest language still needs B.

         Do not block B on C. Do not re-solve A while building B.

         ==========================================================
         1. WHY EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         Today every lambda looks the same:
           (lambda (x) (* x x))              ; pure
           (lambda () (get 'note id))        ; read DB
           (lambda () (create 'note "{}"))   ; write DB
           (lambda () (propose-function ...)); AI + write code
           (lambda () (render-html "..."))   ; page-ish

         The OS cares which is which. Effects make that
         first-class, inspectable, enforceable.

         ==========================================================
         2. EFFECT LATTICE (capability sets)
         ==========================================================

         Base labels (finite, small):

           Pure      no capability prims; only pure computation
           ReadDb    get, rows, select-for-update, list-branches, ...
           WriteDb   create, update, update-field, delete, deftype, ...
           Branch    fork, merge, checkout
           Publish   route (and later define-page helpers)
           Ai        propose-function / LLM client
           Html      render-html (soft; display intent, not security)
           Txn       body expects with-transaction scope


         A function's effect set is a SET of labels, e.g.
           {ReadDb, Html}
           {WriteDb, Ai}
           {}

         Ordering for policy (allowlist checks):
           Pure ⊂ anything
           ReadDb ⊂ ReadDb∪WriteDb  (writes imply reads usually)
           optional sugar: Db = ReadDb∪WriteDb

         NOTE on (define ...):
           Top-level define persists code — a code-store write
           performed by the session layer, NOT an effect tracked
           in the lattice. Distinguish:
             - effects OF a function body (what it does when called)
             - effects OF defining it (session install + persist)

         ==========================================================
         3. PRIM EFFECT TABLE (initial map)
         ==========================================================

           Pure:
             + - * = < > <= >= not
             list car cdr cons null? pair? count
             string-append str type-of
             dict-get dict-set dict-keys
             hx-get hx-post          ; string tags only

           ReadDb:
             get rows select-for-update
             list-branches current-branch list-bindings
             whoami current-author   ; session read; treat ReadDb or soft Ambient

           WriteDb:
             create update update-field delete deftype

           Branch:
             fork merge checkout

           Publish:
             route

           Html:
             render-html

           Ai:
             propose-function
             (Ai usually pairs with WriteDb on code store via define path)

           Txn-related:
             with-transaction (special form) introduces Txn scope
             select-for-update meaningful only under Txn

         Image layer owns the table (primitives live in image/).
         Lisp package may define the effect datatype only.

         ==========================================================
         4. INFERENCE (syntactic, conservative)
         ==========================================================

         effects(expr) =
           union over subexpressions
           + effects of any prim head used in App
           + effects of known global bindings referenced
             (lookup function_defs metadata / env annotation)

         Rules of thumb:
           - overapproximate: if unsure, add effect
           - higher-order unknown fn => Effects.top or {ReadDb,WriteDb,Ai,Publish,Branch} policy knob
           - lambda body effects = function effects
           - quote bodies contribute no runtime effects
           - quasiquote: only unquote / unquote-splicing
             subexpressions contribute; template skeleton is Pure
           - infer on EXPANDED bodies when macros are present
             (lisp-interpreter.macros); macro heads themselves
             are not prims

         On successful (define name body):
           store inferred effect set next to function
           (new column function_defs.effects jsonb OR side table OR
            derive on the fly from body AST — prefer derive-on-read
            for v1 to avoid migration, cache later)

         v1 implementation preference:
           derive-on-read from AST + prim table (no schema change)
           optional cache field later

         ==========================================================
         5. GATES / POLICY (where effects pay rent)
         ==========================================================

         a) UI badges
            /bindings and editor show: pure | db-read | db-write | ai | page

         b) AI propose policy (default)
            allowed = {Pure, ReadDb, Html}
            denied  = {WriteDb, Branch, Publish, Ai} unless user elevates
            (tune experimentally; document in product-experience)

         c) Route publish policy (optional v1)
            (route path fn) warns or rejects if fn has Branch/Ai
            pages should be ReadDb/Html/Pure by default

         d) Sandbox eval (future)
            browser anonymous do-it: Pure only

         e) Transactions
            if body has select-for-update or multi WriteDb,
            linter suggests with-transaction

         ==========================================================
         6. ASYNC STORY (what is already true)
         ==========================================================

         Solved:
           - eval returns value Lwt.t
           - prim_fn = value list -> value Lwt.t
           - composition of DB reads in Lisp works
           - with-transaction threads dynamic connection

         Not the same as effects:
           Lwt answers "when / how concurrency is scheduled"
           Effects answer "what capabilities are required"

         Example:
           (get 'note id) is BOTH
             async (returns promise through eval)
             AND ReadDb (capability)

         You already need both; only async exists today.

         ==========================================================
         7. RELATION TO OCAML 5 EFFECTS
         ==========================================================

         OCaml 5 algebraic effects MAY later implement host-side
         intercept for DB/AI inside the interpreter (handlers).
         That is a runtime rewrite of image/lisp internals.

         Explicit non-goals for first effects delivery:
           - do not replace Lwt in Mirage/httpaf/pgx stack
           - do not expose Effect.perform to Lisp users
           - do not require monadic syntax in Lisp

         Guest programs keep looking like normal Lisp.
         Effects are metadata + checks, not new call syntax
         (unless we add optional declare forms later).

         Optional sugar later:
           (define-pure name body)
           (define-page path body)
         Desugar to define + check inferred ⊆ declared.

         ==========================================================
         8. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 0 (docs/UI copy): explain Pure vs DB vs AI in /about
         Phase 1: Effect module + prim -> effect set table
         Phase 2: infer_effects : expr -> EffectSet.t
         Phase 3: badges on /bindings + /edit + inspect
         Phase 4: AI propose allowlist gate (configurable)
         Phase 5: route publish warnings
         Phase 6: session capability masks + ENFORCEMENT
           (see capability-enforcement; language-roadmap Phase 3)
         Phase 7 (optional research): OCaml effect handlers under eval

         NOTE: Phases 1–5 of THIS effects subsection are largely
         done (table, infer, badges, AI gate). Roadmap Phase 3 is
         about turning metadata into hard gates on public routes
         and agent sessions — not redoing inference.

         ==========================================================
         9. DATA SHAPES (sketch)
         ==========================================================

         type effect =
           | Pure | ReadDb | WriteDb | Branch
           | Publish | Ai | Html | Txn

         type effect_set = effect list  (* unique sorted *)

         val prim_effects : string -> effect_set
         val infer : prim_table_meta -> expr -> effect_set
         val subset : effect_set -> effect_set -> bool
         val pp : effect_set -> string  (* "pure" / "read-db+html" *)

         No change to value or eval types required for Phases 1-5.

         ==========================================================
         10. SUCCESS CRITERIA
         ==========================================================

         - User can see whether a function is pure or touches DB/AI
         - AI-proposed functions can be policy-gated by effects
         - Pure functions remain testable without Postgres
         - Async composition remains intact (no regression of Lwt ABI)
         - Spec/docs stop conflating async with effects
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection binding-sugar
   (doc "Define and body ergonomics: function define, begin/multi-body")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "(define (f x) (+ x 1)) installs lambda; (f 2) => 3")
    (test "multi-body define evaluates to last form")
    (test "function-source prefers short form when body is lambda")
    (smoke "load-library still accepts old (define name (lambda …)) form"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 2.

         ==========================================================
         FUNCTION DEFINE SUGAR
         ==========================================================

           (define (name param…) body body* …)
             ≡  (define name (lambda (param…) (begin body body* …)))

           Single body may omit begin.
           Rest args: only if lambda already supports dotted lists;
             else out of scope for this subsection.

           MUST remain valid:
             (define name expr)           ; values, not only fns
             (define name (lambda …))     ; explicit lambda

         ==========================================================
         BEGIN / MULTI-BODY
         ==========================================================

           (begin e1 e2 … en) => value of en; e1…e(n-1) for effect.
           Implement as seq chain or dedicated special form.
           Alias: if seq is the kernel name, begin is user sugar
           (prefer exporting begin for Scheme familiarity).

           Lambda / define-function bodies accept multiple exprs
           by desugaring to begin.

         ==========================================================
         PRINT PREFERENCE (optional same phase)
         ==========================================================
           function-source / pp may print
             (define (f x) …)
           when stored body is Lambda — cosmetic only; AST store
           stays Define(name, Lambda(…)).

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Internal define (define in middle of body) — later
           - let function sugar (let ((f (x) …)) …) — later
           - defmacro (name p…) sugar — do with define sugar pattern
             if cheap; else follow-up

         SUCCESS: new package code can look like ordinary Scheme
         procedures; old libs keep working without rewrite.

         Docstrings on define are specified in define-docstring
         (orthogonal; may land before or with this sugar).
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection define-docstring
   (doc "Optional docstring on define; persist to function_defs.doc; (function-doc name)")
   (implements lisp/lib/eval.ml lisp/lib/special_forms.ml image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/agent_tools.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build && dune runtest")
    (test "(define d1 1 \"one\") binds 1; (function-doc \"d1\") => \"one\"")
    (test "(define (inc x) \"add one\" (+ x 1)); (inc 2) => 3; function-doc has docstring")
    (test "(define f (lambda (x) \"lam\" (+ x 1))); function-doc and body without dead string")
    (test "redefine without docstring clears or leaves prior doc per documented rule")
    (test "(function-doc \"missing\") => ()")
    (test "old two-arg define still works; load-library packages without docs")
    (smoke "function_defs.doc round-trips across persist + lookup")
    (smoke "fork/merge copy doc with body"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE
           Make every binding documentable in-language so agents
           and humans can build a live reference (apropos, /ref,
           platform-ref notes) without a parallel docs system.
           Inbox SPEC "define docstring" is the product brief;
           this subsection is the book authority for the OCaml
           kernel half. Lisp package doc coverage, /ref, and
           apropos are follow-on product work once this lands.

         SCHEMA (already present — no migration)
           function_defs.doc text  — see storage.schema /
           migrations/001_branches.sql. Column exists; writers
           and readers historically ignored it. This work wires
           it. Do NOT add a second description column.

         SURFACE SYNTAX (all optional doc)
           1. (define name expr)
                no docstring; bind eval(expr)
           2. (define name expr "doc")
                bind eval(expr); doc = the string literal
           3. (define (name p…) body…)
                sugar → lambda; no doc unless (4)
           4. (define (name p…) "doc" body body*…)
                leading string literal in the body list is the
                docstring and is NOT part of the evaluated body
                (requires at least one real body form after it;
                 a lone string body is a value-returning thunk,
                 not a docstring)
           5. (define name (lambda (p…) "doc" body…))
                same leading-string rule inside lambda bodies
                when the define is top-level: strip into doc and
                store/bind the lambda WITHOUT the dead string so
                function-source stays clean

           Docstring MUST be a string literal (Lit VStr / reader
           "…"). A computed expression in the doc slot is a
           syntax error (User_error), not silently evaluated.

         EVAL (pure lisp/)
           - Accept forms (1)–(5) without error.
           - Binding value is unchanged vs pre-docstring define.
           - Pure env does not store docs (no env metadata in v1).
           - Prefer one shared helper (e.g. split_define /
             peel_docstring) used by eval and by the image
             intercept so sugar and doc rules cannot drift.
           - value_to_expr / quote of Define need not round-trip
             the doc string inside the AST (doc is store metadata,
             not part of expr). Body AST after peel has no doc.

         IMAGE PERSIST
           session eval_one / eval_form define intercept and
           agent_tools.define_binding:
             - peel name, body_expr, doc option
             - eval as today (env bind)
             - Store.persist_function … ?doc
           Redefine rules:
             - docstring present → write that string to doc
             - docstring absent → set doc NULL (explicit clear).
               Rationale: edit/save and load-library without docs
               must not leave stale blurbs from an older body.
               Packages that care keep the string in source.
           Anonymous may not persist (unchanged).
           defmacro: out of scope for v1 docstring slot (no
           third-arg / leading-string capture); follow-up if needed.

         STORE
           persist_function : t -> name -> body -> author ->
             ?doc:string option -> unit Lwt.t
           INSERT/UPDATE sets doc (NULL when None).
           lookup_function / list paths that feed function-doc
           must SELECT fd.doc (nullable).
           fork copy, merge upsert, promote_functions: copy doc
           with body/author so docs survive branch ops.

         PRIMITIVE (function-doc name) → string | ()
           - name: string (binding name)
           - If visible function_defs row has non-null non-empty
             doc → VStr doc
           - Else if name is a special form → that form's summary
             from special_forms catalog (discoverability)
           - Else if name is a registered prim with a help blurb
             → that blurb when one exists
           - Else VNil
           Effect class: ReadDb (store lookup); pure fallbacks
           after miss are fine inside the same prim.
           Register next to function-source / function-ast.
           package-exports kernel/std list should include
           function-doc once shipped.

         SPECIAL-FORMS CATALOG
           Update define entry arity/examples to show optional
           docstring forms.

         NON-GOALS (this subsection)
           - /ref page, apropos, nav link, ai/context wiring
             (product docs layer; build after kernel works)
           - Mass docstring backfill of libs/*.pp
           - doc on type_defs or routes
           - Markdown rendering of docs in OCaml UI (plain text)
           - Changing function_defs PRIMARY KEY or body JSON shape

         SUCCESS
           - One define with a docstring round-trips through
             persist and (function-doc …)
           - Sugar and lambda leading-string forms agree
           - Existing libs without docs load unchanged
           - Agents can document bindings in-language and read
             them back without scraping function-source comments
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection match
   (doc "Pattern match / destructuring for lists and code-as-data trees")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "match list pattern binds vars")
    (test "match symbol literal ('lambda) works with VSymbol")
    (test "match fallthrough / else")
    (smoke "read-string tree walked with match not only car/cdr"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 2. Depends on Phase 1 symbols so patterns
         can match symbolic heads honestly.

         ==========================================================
         WHY
         ==========================================================
           Agents and macros spend their lives on trees.
           car/cdr chains are correct but hostile. match is the
           highest-leverage ergonomics feature after symbols.

         ==========================================================
         SURFACE (v1 sketch — lock in plan before make)
         ==========================================================

           (match scrutinee
             (pat1 body1…)
             (pat2 body2…)
             (_     else-body…))

         Patterns (minimum set):
           _                 wildcard
           x                 bind variable (symbol)
           lit               number / bool / nil / string
           'sym              quoted symbol literal (VSymbol)
           "str"             string literal
           (p1 p2 …)         proper list of patterns
           (p1 p2 … . rest)  optional dotted tail if pairs allow

         Optional v1.1:
           (? pred p)        predicate guard
           (and p1 p2)
           (or p1 p2)

         Non-goals v1:
           - record/struct patterns
           - exhaustiveness checking
           - Racket-style syntax-parse
           - regexp patterns

         ==========================================================
         SEMANTICS
         ==========================================================
           First matching clause wins.
           No match => User_error kind match_error (catchable).
           Bindings visible in clause body (lexical).
           Scrutinee evaluated once.

         Implementation sketch:
           special form in eval; compile patterns to decision tree
           or linear backtracking — linear fine for v1.

         ==========================================================
         SUCCESS
         ==========================================================
           Idiomatic AST walk:
             (match form
               ((list 'lambda params body) …)
               ((list 'define name body) …)
               (_ …))
           works with symbols from read-string/function-ast.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection std-library
   (doc "Blessed std list/string helpers: freeze surface for packages and agents")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "libs/verify or lisp tests cover each frozen std name")
    (smoke "export-package std lists frozen names; load-library std idempotent"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 2.

         ==========================================================
         PROBLEM
         ==========================================================
           list/map, list/foldl, string helpers, etc. accreted in
           libs/std and OCaml prims without a freeze. Agents invent
           duplicates; renames break gardens.

         ==========================================================
         RULES
         ==========================================================
           1. Document the FROZEN SET in this subsection (table).
           2. Frozen names: behavior may bugfix; signatures must
              not change without a new name (map2, etc.).
           3. Prefer OCaml prims for hot loops; Lisp std may wrap.
           4. New helpers go to std only with a verify case.

         ==========================================================
         FROZEN SET (initial — adjust at make time to match code)
         ==========================================================
           Lists:
             list/map list/filter list/foldl list/foldr?
             reverse append length/count nth
             null? pair? car cdr cons list
           Strings:
             string-append string-length substring string-eq
             string-contains? string-trim html-escape
             as-str str
           Symbols (after Phase 1):
             symbol? string? symbol=? symbol->string string->symbol
           Misc:
             not type-of

         html/* and layout/* are packages, not kernel std freeze
         (they may evolve faster) — but should prefer frozen std.

         ==========================================================
         SUCCESS
         ==========================================================
           One page in concepts or /bindings kernel docs lists the
           freeze; verify suite fails if a frozen name disappears.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection capability-enforcement
   (doc "Hard gates: public routes and agent sessions obey effect allowlists")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "public route with WriteDb+Branch handler rejected or forced private")
    (smoke "agent session with ReadDb-only mask cannot create data row via tool")
    (smoke "admin private routes still allow full effects")
    (test "subset checks unit-tested on effect sets"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 3. Builds on effects inference (implemented).

         ==========================================================
         PROBLEM
         ==========================================================
           Effects are visible (badges) and partly used (AI propose).
           Public routes and agent tools still need HARD policy:
           metadata without enforcement is documentation.

         ==========================================================
         SURFACES TO GATE
         ==========================================================

           1) User routes with visibility=public
                When (route path handler 'public) or equivalent:
                infer_effects(handler) must be ⊆ PUBLIC_ALLOW.
                Default PUBLIC_ALLOW:
                  Pure, ReadDb, Html
                  (optional: WriteDb only with explicit opt-in flag
                   later — default OFF for v1 enforcement)
                Forbidden by default: Branch, Ai, Publish-of-nested,
                  Txn (unless explicitly allowed).
                On violate: refuse to mark public (keep private) OR
                403 at serve time — prefer refuse at route-bind.

           2) Agent sessions / tool loops
                Session or agent-session carries effect_mask.
                Each tool prim call: prim_effects ⊆ mask.
                Default agent mask: ReadDb ∪ Pure ∪ Html ∪ Ai
                  (no Branch merge to system; no admin auth prims).
                Configurable per session seed for trusted agents.

           3) AI propose-function (already gated — keep + unify
                on the same subset helper).

           4) Optional: eval budget already hard — document as
                part of the same "production leash" story.

         ==========================================================
         MECHANICS
         ==========================================================
           - Single OCaml helper: allow : got:effect_set -> mask -> bool
           - infer at define/route time; cache on binding meta if cheap
           - Fail closed on unknown prim (treat as max capability
             or reject) — pick reject-unknown for public surface

         ==========================================================
         AUTH INTERACTION
         ==========================================================
           Enforcement is orthogonal to is_admin:
             admin on private branch: full powers
             admin publishing public route: still PUBLIC_ALLOW
             anonymous hitting public route: handler already constrained

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Full setuid / run-as-author lattice (effects § phase 6 note)
           - Per-row RLS in Postgres as substitute for effects
           - User-authored effect declarations required (inference first;
             optional declare later)

         SUCCESS: a malicious or sloppy public handler cannot fork
         branches or call admin create-user; agent tools respect mask.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection package-surface
   (doc "Blessed package exports and stability rules for system/main")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (smoke "export-package or package-bindings lists public names for std/ui")
    (smoke "docs or /bindings marks public vs internal"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ROADMAP: Phase 3 (ecosystem law, light).

         ==========================================================
         PROBLEM
         ==========================================================
           system/main packages are de facto public API for all
           gardens. No export list → agents depend on internals;
           renames break personal branches silently.

         ==========================================================
         v1 RULES (no heavy module system)
         ==========================================================
           1. Each libs/<pkg>/lib.pp MAY start with a declaration:
                (package pkg-name
                  (export name1 name2 …))
              If absent, all defines are public (status quo).
              If present, non-exported names are "internal":
                still callable (Lisp-1) but documented as unstable;
                optional lint/warn on other packages referencing them.

           2. Stability tiers:
                kernel prims     — strongest freeze (with std-library)
                exported package — prefer compat; rename = new name
                internal         — may change any deploy
                personal branch  — user owns breakage

           3. Promote discipline (existing branching.promote):
                only exported names should be promoted to main
                without explicit force.

           4. Agent seeds: prefer exported names only.

         ==========================================================
         NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Import forms / separate namespaces per file
           - Version numbers in-package (git/branch is version)
           - Sealing that makes internal uncallable (too hostile
             for a Lisp OS v1)

         SUCCESS: std + ui + layout have export lists; concepts
         doc explains public vs internal; agents stop depending
         on clearly internal helpers by default.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
